 "drivingQuestion": "How do we know 'shilu'ach' denotes growth?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the verse shows shilu'ach means growth",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "nazir.3a.7",
   "daf": "3a",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "״הֲרֵי עָלַי לְשַׁלֵּחַ פֶּרַע״ — הֲרֵי זֶה נָזִיר. מִמַּאי דְּהָדֵין שִׁלּוּחַ רִיבּוּיָא הוּא, דִּכְתִיב: ״שְׁלָחַיִךְ פַּרְדֵּס רִמּוֹנִים״.",
   "en": "The mishna rules that with regard to one who says: «It is» incumbent «upon me to grow [leshale’aḥ] long hair, he is a nazirite.» The Gemara asks: «From where» is it known «that this» term «shilu’aḥ,» which is a different form of the word leshale’aḥ, «is» an expression of «increase» and growth? The Gemara answers: «As it is written: “Your tresses [shelaḥayikh] are a park of pomegranates”» (Song of Songs 4:13)."
  }
 ],
